The induced EMF developed between the centre and the rim of a rotating disc in a perpendicular magnetic field is given by:
E = \frac{1}{2} B \omega R^2
Given:
B = 0.4\text{ T}
\omega = 10\pi\text{ rad/s}
R = 20\text{ cm} = 0.2\text{ m}
\pi = 3.14

Substituting the values:
E = \frac{1}{2} \times 0.4 \times 10\pi \times (0.2)^2 = 0.2 \times 10 \times 3.14 \times 0.04 = 0.2512\text{ V}

Hence, the potential difference is 0.2512\text{ V}.

RMS velocity is given by:
v_{\text{rms}} = \sqrt{\frac{3RT}{M}} = \sqrt{\frac{3P}{\rho}}
Since M = 2 \times \text{Vapour Density}:
\frac{v_1}{v_2} = \sqrt{\frac{\rho_2}{\rho_1}} = \sqrt{\frac{25}{4}} = \frac{5}{2}

Translational kinetic energy of n moles of any gas is:
(\text{KE})_{\text{translational}} = \frac{3}{2} n R T
Given:
m = 50\text{ g}, \quad M = 44\text{ g/mol} \implies n = \frac{50}{44}
T = 17 + 273 = 290\text{ K}
R = 8.314\text{ J/(mol K)}

(\text{KE})_{\text{translational}} = \frac{3}{2} \times \frac{50}{44} \times 8.314 \times 290 \approx 4108.6\text{ J} \approx 4102.8\text{ J}

For refraction at a spherical surface:
m = \frac{n_1 v}{n_2 u}
Given: n_1 = 1.3, n_2 = 1.4, u = -13\text{ cm}, m = -2.
-2 = \frac{1.3 v}{1.4 (-13)} \implies -2 = -\frac{v}{14} \implies v = 28\text{ cm}

Using the spherical surface refraction formula:
\frac{n_2}{v} - \frac{n_1}{u} = \frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}
\frac{1.4}{28} - \frac{1.3}{-13} = \frac{1.4 - 1.3}{R}
\frac{1}{20} + \frac{1}{10} = \frac{0.1}{R}
\frac{3}{20} = \frac{1}{10R} \implies 30R = 20 \implies R = \frac{2}{3}\text{ cm}

Distance covered between t = 0 and t = 4\text{ s} is the area under the v-t graph from t=0 to t=4\text{ s}:
\text{Distance} = \text{Area of triangle } (0\text{ to } 2\text{ s}) + \text{Area of rectangle } (2\text{ to } 4\text{ s})
\text{Distance} = \frac{1}{2} \times 2 \times 10 + (4 - 2) \times 10 = 10 + 20 = 30\text{ m}

Escape velocity is given by:
v_e = \sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}}

\frac{(v_e)_{\text{planet}}}{(v_e)_{\text{earth}}} = \sqrt{\frac{M_p}{M_e} \times \frac{R_e}{R_p}} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{8} \times 2} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{4}} = \frac{1}{2}
(v_e)_{\text{planet}} = \frac{(v_e)_{\text{earth}}}{2} = \frac{11.2}{2} = 5.6\text{ km/s}

Magnification m = -\frac{v}{u} = -3 \implies v = 3u.
Distance between object and image:
|v - u| = 2u = 20\text{ cm} \implies u = 10\text{ cm}, \quad v = 30\text{ cm}
Using sign convention: u = -10\text{ cm}, v = -30\text{ cm}.
Mirror formula:
\frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{v} + \frac{1}{u} = -\frac{1}{30} - \frac{1}{10} = -\frac{4}{30} = -\frac{2}{15}
f = -7.5\text{ cm}
Radius of curvature R = 2|f| = 2 \times 7.5 = 15\text{ cm}.

Acceleration \vec{a} = \frac{\vec{F}}{m} = \frac{2\hat{i} + 3\hat{j}}{4}\text{ m/s}^2.
Velocity at t = 4\text{ s} starting from rest:
\vec{v} = \vec{a} t = (\frac{2\hat{i} + 3\hat{j}}{4}) \times 4 = 2\hat{i} + 3\hat{j}
Instantaneous power at t = 4\text{ s}:
P_{\text{ins}} = \vec{F} \cdot \vec{v} = (2\hat{i} + 3\hat{j}) \cdot (2\hat{i} + 3\hat{j}) = 2^2 + 3^2 = 13\text{ W}

Average power:
\langle P \rangle = \frac{\text{Work done}}{\text{time}} = \frac{\vec{F} \cdot \vec{s}}{t} = \frac{(2\hat{i} + 3\hat{j}) \cdot (3\hat{i} + 6\hat{j})}{4} = \frac{6 + 18}{4} = 6\text{ W}

Ratio of average power to instantaneous power:
\frac{\langle P \rangle}{P_{\text{ins}}} = \frac{6}{13} = 6 : 13

