Let \vec{c} = \hat{i} + \hat{j}.
\vec{a} \times \vec{c} = (2\hat{i} + \hat{j} - \hat{k}) \times (\hat{i} + \hat{j}) = \hat{i} - \hat{j} + \hat{k}
(\vec{a} \times \vec{c}) \times \hat{i} = (\hat{i} - \hat{j} + \hat{k}) \times \hat{i} = \hat{j} + \hat{k}
\vec{b} = (\hat{j} + \hat{k}) \times \hat{i} = -\hat{k} + \hat{j} = \hat{j} - \hat{k}

Projection of \vec{a} on \vec{b}:
\text{proj} = \frac{\vec{a} \cdot \vec{b}}{|\vec{b}|} = \frac{(2\hat{i} + \hat{j} - \hat{k}) \cdot (\hat{j} - \hat{k})}{\sqrt{1^2 + (-1)^2}} = \frac{1 + 1}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{2}{\sqrt{2}} = \sqrt{2}
Square of projection = (\sqrt{2})^2 = 2.

1. \sin(2\tan^{-1}(1/3)):
Let \theta = \tan^{-1}(1/3) \implies \tan\theta = 1/3.
\sin(2\theta) = \frac{2\tan\theta}{1 + \tan^2\theta} = \frac{2(1/3)}{1 + 1/9} = \frac{2/3}{10/9} = \frac{3}{5}

2. \cos(\tan^{-1}(2\sqrt{2})):
Let \phi = \tan^{-1}(2\sqrt{2}) \implies \tan\phi = 2\sqrt{2} = \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{1}.
\cos\phi = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + (2\sqrt{2})^2}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + 8}} = \frac{1}{3}

