1. Coefficients \binom{12}{r-1}, \binom{12}{r}, \binom{12}{r+1} in G.P.:
(\binom{12}{r})^2 = \binom{12}{r-1} \binom{12}{r+1}
\frac{\binom{12}{r}}{\binom{12}{r-1}} = \frac{\binom{12}{r+1}}{\binom{12}{r}} \implies \frac{13 - r}{r} = \frac{12 - r}{r + 1}
(13 - r)(r + 1) = r(12 - r) \implies 13 + 12r - r^2 = 12r - r^2 \implies 13 = 0 \text{ (No integer solution)}
Thus p = 0.

2. General term of (3^{1/4} + 4^{1/3})^{12}:
T_{k+1} = \binom{12}{k} (3^{1/4})^{12-k} (4^{1/3})^k = \binom{12}{k} 3^{\frac{12-k}{4}} 4^{\frac{k}{3}}
For rational terms, k must be a multiple of 3 and 12-k must be a multiple of 4.
k \in {0, 12}:
- For k = 0: T_1 = \binom{12}{0} 3^3 4^0 = 1 \times 27 \times 1 = 27
- For k = 12: T_{13} = \binom{12}{12} 3^0 4^4 = 1 \times 1 \times 256 = 256
Sum q = 27 + 256 = 283.

p + q = 0 + 283 = 283
